WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:04.560
Dear Fellow Scholars, this is Two Minute&nbsp;
Papers with Dr. Károly Zsolnai-Fehér.

00:00:04.560 --> 00:00:12.560
Today we are going to torment a virtual&nbsp;
armadillo, become happy, or sad depending on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:12.560 --> 00:00:19.840
which way we are bending, create a ton of jelly&nbsp;
sandwiches, design a crazy bridge, and more.

00:00:20.560 --> 00:00:28.000
So, what is going on here? Well, this new paper&nbsp;
helps us enhance our physics simulation programs&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:34.320
by improving how we evaluate derivatives.&nbsp;
Derivatives describe how things change.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:35.040 --> 00:00:42.800
Now, of course, evaluating derivatives is not new.&nbsp;
It’s not even old. It is ancient. Here you see&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.800 --> 00:00:50.880
an ancient technique for this, and it works well&nbsp;
most of the time…but whoa! Well, this simulation&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:50.880 --> 00:00:59.200
blew up in our face, so, yes, it may work well&nbsp;
most of the time, but unfortunately, not here.

00:00:59.200 --> 00:01:03.680
Now, if you are wondering what should&nbsp;
be happening in this scene. Here is the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:03.680 --> 00:01:08.560
reference simulation that showcases&nbsp;
what should have happened. Yes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.560 --> 00:01:13.760
this is the famous pastime of the computer&nbsp;
graphics researcher, tormenting virtual&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:13.760 --> 00:01:20.400
objects basically all day long. So, I hope&nbsp;
you are enjoying this reference simulation.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.400 --> 00:01:26.960
Because this is a great reference simulation.&nbsp;
It is as reference as a simulation can get.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.680 --> 00:01:33.120
Now, I hope you know what’s coming. Hold on to&nbsp;
your papers, because this is not the reference&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:33.120 --> 00:01:39.840
simulation. What you see here is the new technique&nbsp;
described in this paper. Absolutely amazing.

00:01:40.480 --> 00:01:46.080
Actually, let’s compare the two. This is the&nbsp;
reference simulation, for real this time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:47.440 --> 00:01:51.520
and this is the new, complex&nbsp;
step finite difference method.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.520 --> 00:01:57.760
The two are so close that they are&nbsp;
essentially the same. I love it. So good.

00:01:58.320 --> 00:02:05.280
Now, if the comparison made you hungry, of course,&nbsp;
we can proceed to the jelly sandwiches. Here is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.280 --> 00:02:13.280
the same scene simulated with a bunch of previous&nbsp;
techniques, and…my goodness! All of them look&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.280 --> 00:02:20.080
different! So, which of these jelly sandwiches&nbsp;
is better? Well, the new technique is better,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:20.080 --> 00:02:26.640
because this is the only one that preserves volume&nbsp;
properly. This is the one that gets us the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.640 --> 00:02:33.280
jelly. With each of the other methods, the jelly&nbsp;
either reacts incorrectly, or at the end of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.280 --> 00:02:40.320
simulation, there is less jelly than the amount&nbsp;
we started with. Now, you are probably asking,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:40.320 --> 00:02:48.560
is this really possible? Yes, yes it is! What’s&nbsp;
more, this is not only possible, but this is a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:48.560 --> 00:02:54.160
widespread problem in physics simulation. Our&nbsp;
seasoned Fellow Scholars had seen this problem&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.160 --> 00:03:01.200
in many previous episodes, for instance, here is&nbsp;
one with the tragedy of the disappearing bunnies.

00:03:01.200 --> 00:03:07.600
And, preserving the volume of the simulated&nbsp;
materials is not only useful for jelly sandwiches.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:07.600 --> 00:03:15.280
It is also useful for doing extreme yoga. Look -&nbsp;
here are a bunch of previous techniques trying to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:15.280 --> 00:03:23.280
simulate this, and, what do we see? Extreme&nbsp;
bending, extreme bending… and more extreme&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:32.160
bending. Good I guess? Well, not quite! This yoga&nbsp;
shouldn’t be nearly as extreme as we see here. The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:32.160 --> 00:03:38.160
new technique reveals that this kind of bending&nbsp;
shouldn’t happen given these material properties.

00:03:38.800 --> 00:03:44.240
And wait, here comes one of my favorites.&nbsp;
The new technique can also deal with this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.240 --> 00:03:52.160
crazy example. Look, a nice little virtual&nbsp;
hyperelastic material, where the bending energy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:59.920
changes depending on the bending orientation,&nbsp;
revealing a secret. Or, two secrets, as you see,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:59.920 --> 00:04:06.080
it does not like bending to the right so much,&nbsp;
but bending towards the left, now we’re talking!

00:04:06.720 --> 00:04:12.080
And, it can also help us perform&nbsp;
inverse design problems. For instance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.080 --> 00:04:20.080
here we have a hyperelastic bridge built from&nbsp;
over 20 thousand parts. And here, we can design&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:20.080 --> 00:04:24.480
what vibration frequencies should be&nbsp;
present when the wind blows at our bridge.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:25.120 --> 00:04:31.680
And here comes the coolest part - we can choose&nbsp;
this in advance, and then, the new technique&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:36.960
quickly finds the suitable geometry that&nbsp;
will showcase the prescribed vibration types.

00:04:38.000 --> 00:04:44.000
It pretty much converges after 4 to 6&nbsp;
Newton iterations. What does that mean?&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:48.720
Yes, it means that the new technique&nbsp;
comes up with an initial guess,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:55.840
and it needs to refine it only 4 to 6 times&nbsp;
until it comes up with an excellent solution.

00:04:56.640 --> 00:05:03.200
So, better hyperelastic simulations,&nbsp;
quickly and conveniently? Yes please,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.200 --> 00:05:09.040
sign me up right now! And, what would you use&nbsp;
this for? Let me know in the comments below!

00:05:56.800 --> 00:06:00.880
Thanks for watching and for your generous&nbsp;
support, and I'll see you next time!

